.TH smi-query-db "1" "November 2026" "SMI Toolset" "SMI Base Command"
.SH NAME
smi-query-db \- Run SQL against an H2, Derby, SQLite or PostgreSQL database, one statement, a script or interactively
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B smi-query-db
.B h2|derby|sqlite|postgres
.RI [ VARIABLE = value ...]
.RI [ SQL ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.PP
.B smi-query-db
connects to the database of the type given as the sub command, with the
.B SMI_DB_
variables that
.BR smi-create-db (1)
prints and
.BR smi-liquibase (1)
reads, and runs SQL with the client of that database:
.TP
.I SQL
given: that one statement, its result as a table.
.TP
standard input not a terminal: the SQL script read from it, statements ended with
.BR ; ,
each shown with its result, stopping at the first error.
.TP
standard input a terminal: an interactive SQL shell.
.PP
.BR h2 ,
.B derby
and
.B sqlite
run through the JDBC tools of H2,
.B org.h2.tools.Shell
and
.BR org.h2.tools.RunScript ,
with every driver of the
.I jdbc
directory of the SMI lib directory, so one client works for all of them.
.B postgres
runs
.BR psql (1).
.PP
Unlike
.BR smi-liquibase ,
it takes no Liquibase lock, so any user of the database can use it, the application and read only
users as well, with exactly the rights that user has.
.PP
A database that does not exist is never created: H2 is opened with
.BR IFEXISTS=TRUE ,
a Derby URL has no
.BR create=true ,
and a missing SQLite file is an error.
.SH SUB COMMANDS
.TP
.B h2
User default
.BR sa .
Needs the
.B h2
package.
.TP
.B derby
User default
.BR app ,
which selects the schema
.BR APP .
Needs the
.B derby
and
.B h2
packages.
.TP
.B sqlite
No user or password. Needs the
.B sqlitejdbc
and
.B h2
packages.
.TP
.B postgres
Needs
.BR psql .
.SH ARGUMENTS
.TP
.IR VARIABLE = value
Sets one of the variables below for this run, the same as setting it in the environment. The
first argument that is not a variable of the sub command starts the SQL; an
.B SMI_DB_
variable the sub command does not take is an error.
.TP
.I SQL
The statement. More arguments are joined with spaces, so quoting the whole statement is only
needed for the characters of the shell.
.SH VARIABLES
.SS h2, derby, sqlite
.TP
.B SMI_DB_URL
The JDBC URL, as printed by
.BR smi-create-db .
.TP
.BR SMI_DB_DIR ", " SMI_DB_NAME
Used when
.B SMI_DB_URL
is not set: the database
.I NAME
in the directory, default the current one, named as
.B smi-create-db
names it.
.TP
.BR SMI_DB_USER ", " SMI_DB_PASSWORD
User and password.
.TP
.BR JAVA_HOME ", " JAVA_OPTS
The Java to run and its options.
.SS postgres
.TP
.BR SMI_DB_HOST ", " SMI_DB_PORT ", " SMI_DB_NAME ", " SMI_DB_USER
Defaults
.BR localhost ,
.BR 5432 ,
.B postgres
and
.BR postgres ,
as in
.BR smi-liquibase .
An empty
.B SMI_DB_HOST
connects through the local Unix socket.
.TP
.B SMI_DB_PASSWORD
Passed as
.BR PGPASSWORD ,
not visible in the process list. Unset,
.B psql
uses
.IR ~/.pgpass ,
peer authentication, or asks.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The SQL ran.
.TP
.B 1
Wrong arguments, an unknown variable, a missing driver, client or database, or an SQL error.
.PP
For
.BR postgres ,
the exit status of
.BR psql .
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Query the database
.BR smi-create-db
made, with the variables it printed:
.RS
.nf
\&. ./shop.env
smi-query-db sqlite "SELECT id, name FROM customer ORDER BY id"
.fi
.RE
.PP
Run a script and keep its output:
.RS
.nf
smi-query-db h2 < reports/monthly.sql > monthly.txt
.fi
.RE
.PP
Query as the application user instead of the structure owner:
.RS
.nf
smi-query-db postgres SMI_DB_USER="$SMI_DB_APP_USER" SMI_DB_PASSWORD="$SMI_DB_APP_PASSWORD" "SELECT count(*) FROM customer"
.fi
.RE
.PP
A Derby database by directory and name, without an environment file:
.RS
.nf
smi-query-db derby SMI_DB_DIR=/srv/data SMI_DB_NAME=orders "SELECT * FROM purchase_order"
.fi
.RE
.PP
An interactive shell:
.RS
.nf
smi-query-db h2
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR psql (1),
.BR smi-create-db (1),
.BR smi-liquibase (1),
.BR smi-neo4j-query (1)
.SH AUTHOR
setmy.info
